e grieve in these uncertain  times  there are families across this country  mourning those who have died of kovat 19  and in those small Nova Scotian  communities where so many people were  murdered it is natural of course a  normal to come together to mourn but  kovat 19 is robbing us of our rituals  and there will be no big memorials no  traditional funerals with me as Pastor  Steven Adams he's from Faith Baptist  Church in great village Nova Scotia  that's just down the road from porta  pick pastor Adams first our condolences  to everyone in the communities there the  grief must be so acute and this is small  town Nova Scotia everyone is closely  connected how are people coping people I  think are still in shock and which I  think most people who have gone through  any kind of trauma or observed people  going through trauma would appreciate  the reality of that it's it is to use  the word like overwhelming would be a  gross understatement the premier says  there can be no mass gatherings to  grieve that Kovach 19 will not pause  because of the pain that must add  heartbreak to the heartbreak I don't  know if it adds to the heartbreak but  it's sure it sure makes it more  difficult to be you know there for other  people and have other people provide you  know comfort there are plans for a  virtual vigil on Friday I know that is  still in the works but do you know what  it might look like I'm not part of the  planning committee for that I have been  asked if I would participate and I said  that I would I know that a number of  people have been asked to participate  community people and and and people you  know across the country and the people  here that are impacted so closely you  know who've lost family members there  they're in shock and grief and and so  we're grieving along with them and to  try to just try to wrap our minds around  that is  that's difficult these are these are  very difficult days we're going through  tremendous times of loss and grief but  